 In Django, you cannot directly swap out tables with different column names in your code without making adjustments. However, you can design your models in a more flexible way using Python dictionaries or abstract classes to handle columns with dynamic names. This approach will not eliminate the need for updating the code when changing column names but will make it less tedious and easier to manage.

One common solution is to use a dictionary in your model to map the old column names to the new ones:

1. Create an abstract base model with the common fields as properties using getters and setters:

```python
class BaseModel(models.Model):
    old_column_name = models.CharField(max_length=255)

    class Meta:
        abstract = True

    @property
    def new_column_name(self):
        return self.old_column_name

    @new_column_name.setter
    def new_column_name(self, value):
        self.old_column_name = value
```

2. Define your specific models that inherit from the abstract base model and override the mapping dictionary:

```python
class TableA(BaseModel):
    mapping = {
        'old_column_name': 'new_column_name_in_table_a'
    }

class TableB(BaseModel):
    mapping = {
        'old_column_name': 'new_column_name_in_table_b'
    }
```

