1. The Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand (June 28, 1914, Sarajevo, Bosnia): This event is considered the spark that ignited World War I. Gavrilo Princip, a Serbian nationalist, assassinated Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria-Hungary and his wife Sophie in Sarajevo, Bosnia. Austria-Hungary declared war on Serbia, which led to a chain reaction of alliances being invoked and ultimately the involvement of most European powers. A lesser-known factor contributing to this moment was the complex web of alliances between various European nations, which escalated what could have been a localized conflict into a global war.

2. The Battle of the Marne (September 6–12, 1914, Marne River, France): This battle marked the end of the German advance into France and the beginning of trench warfare. The French and British forces successfully halted the German Schlieffen Plan, which aimed to quickly defeat France before turning to Russia. A lesser-known factor contributing to the outcome of this battle was the poor coordination and communication within the German high command, which resulted in missed opportunities to capitalize on initial successes.

3. The United States' Entry into the War (April 6, 1917): The US initially remained neutral in the conflict but entered the war after Germany resumed unrestricted submarine warfare against Allied ships. This action threatened American commerce and led President Woodrow Wilson to ask Congress for a declaration of war. A lesser-known factor contributing to the US entry into the war was the Zimmerman Telegram, a diplomatic message from Germany to Mexico proposing an alliance against the US. This telegram was intercepted and decoded by British intelligence, leading to public outrage and increased pressure on the US government to take action.

