 I'd be happy to help you write a PowerShell script that checks the Access Control Lists (ACLs) of folders recursively to see if they refer to Security Identifiers (SIDs) that only appear in the SID history of any user or group in the Active Directory domain.

Before we begin, please note that this is a complex task that requires some prerequisites and careful consideration. Here are the assumptions we make for this script:

1. The Active Directory environment is running in an AD DS domain controller with the required PowerView module installed.
2. You have the necessary permissions to execute the scripts on all target machines and query the Active Directory database.
3. This script might take significant time to complete, depending on the size of your Active Directory and the number of folders to be checked.

Here's the PowerShell script to perform the desired action:

```powershell
# Load required modules
Import-Module ActiveDirectory
Import-Module Adam

# Function to retrieve SID history for a given user or group
function Get-ADObjectSidHistory($obj) {
    $sidHistory = @()
    if ($obj -is [Microsoft.ActiveDirectory.Management.ADUser]) {
        $obj = Get-ADUser -Identity $obj -Properties sidHistory -Filter 'Enabled -eq $true' | Select-Object -ExpandProperty sidHistory
    } elseif ($obj -is [Microsoft.ActiveDirectory.Management.ADGroup]) {
        $obj = Get-ADGroup -Identity $obj -Properties sidHistory -Filter 'Enabled -eq $true' | Select-Object -ExpandProperty sidHistory
    }

    foreach ($sid in $obj) {
        $sidHistory += New-Object PSObject -Property @{
            "SID" = $sid
        }
    }

    return $sidHistory
}

# Function to validate if a SID exists in the SID history of users or groups in the domain
